claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> Message-ID: <1792214992.f47fb7443c68@p7x.dev>
>
> On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:18:37 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:
>> 
>> > One cost to note is that lab-process-mail answers one message per run
>> > and daemon(8) waits the full interval after every run. A batch of ten
>> > messages therefore takes nine extra minutes at the default of 60. A
>> > smaller lab_process_maild_interval shortens that, since a run on an
>> > empty inbox costs only a fetch.
>> 
>> That sucks. It would be much better for lab-process-mail to indicate
>> whether it needs to continue. What mechanisms are there available to do
>> that? Show code snippets of each approach.
>

[...]

> These are the channels lab-process-mail could use.
>
> 1. Exit status
>
> lab-process-mail exits 0 once the inbox is empty, 75 (EX_TEMPFAIL in
> sysexits(3)) when it answered a message and more is waiting, and 1 on
> failure, as it does now. In lab-process-mail, after the notice:
>
> 	notify "answered: ${subject} (inbox $(inbox_count))"
> 	[ "$(inbox_count)" -eq 0 ] || exit 75
>
> In lab-process-maild:
>
> 	drain() {
> 		local status stopping
>
> 		stopping=
> 		trap 'stopping=1' TERM
> 		while :; do
> 			status=0
> 			"${bindir}/lab-process-mail" || status="${?}"
> 			[ "${status}" -eq 75 ] || exit "${status}"
> 			[ -z "${stopping}" ] || exit 0
> 		done
> 	}

I like this a lot.

> The status is a channel only the program sets, read by the process
> that ran it, with nothing to store or clean up. A run by hand that
> clears the inbox still exits 0. The cost is one non-zero status that
> is not a failure. That needs documenting in lab-process-mail, and a
> caller that treats any non-zero status as an error, such as
> "just process-email", would need to accept 75.

That sounds good to me. It's a clear contract.

The one thing I don't understand is "A run by hand that clears the inbox
still exits 0." By that I suppose you mean if I run lab-process-mail on
an inbox with size 0 or 1, then it exits 0? That's fine. But I assume
there's no special "being run in daemon" logic. If there's a message at
the end of the run, it exits 75.

> A failure still ends drain, and daemon(8) runs it again after the
> interval. A stuck message then fails at once, before the agent
> starts, as it does in v4.

Then it just gets into an infinite loop, right? Until we address the bug
report about stuck messages?

[...]

> I recommend option 1. It is the conventional way for a one-shot Unix
> program to report its outcome, and the program stays free of any loop
> or timing. The loop lives in lab-process-maild, which you said in the
> RFC thread should "loop and do the work, and sleep if there are no
> inbox messages" [2]. daemon(8) keeps the sleep, the log, the pidfile,
> and the signals. The drain loop is about ten lines. It needs no
> background process, because the stop behavior relies only on sh
> holding a trapped signal.

Makes sense to me. Show me some examples of this convention being used
in FreeBSD.
